      "title": "Cost Benefit Analysis Dashboard (Tableau)",
      "type": "CASE STUDY",
      "summary": "I built a Tableau dashboard analyzing cost and profit performance across products and states, pairing a profit-vs-sales trendline with budget-vs-actual margin tracking over time.",
      "body": "Context: A Tableau assignment with a defined brief: given a sales/cost dataset, build a \"Cost Benefit Analysis Dashboard\" to a specific spec - set KPIs, a scatter chart with trendline, a dual-axis line chart, a dual-axis column-and-line chart, and a state-level map - then submit under my own student ID.\n\nThe problem: Turn raw per-transaction sales/cost data (area code, state, market, margin, sales, COGS, expenses, marketing spend, inventory, and budgeted counterparts for several of these) into a view that shows whether the business is profitable, where, on what, and whether it's hitting its budget.\n\nData / Input: An Excel dataset (\"Sample Data_USA Sales\") with columns including Area Code, State, Market, Market Size, Profit, Margin, Sales, COGS, Total Expenses, Marketing, Inventory, and budgeted counterparts (Budget Profit, Budget COGS, Budget Margin, Budget Sales) - course-provided, not verified real sales data.\n\nApproach: Five worksheets, each mapped to a required visual: a KPI view (I included Total Sales alongside the five the brief specified - Total Profit, Total Revenue, Total COGS, Total Expenses, Average Marketing Cost), a Profit-vs-Sales scatter chart with a trendline, a dual-axis line chart comparing Budgeted Margin against Actual Margin by month, a dual-axis column-and-line chart showing Marketing Cost and Sales per product, and a map showing profit by state.\n\nAnalysis: Two of the five visuals are explicitly dual-axis (the margin trend and the marketing-cost/sales combo), which meant setting up two measures on independent scales against a shared category axis rather than just dropping fields onto shelves the brief specifically calls this out as a technique to use, not just a chart type to produce.\n\nOutput: A working Tableau workbook (Tableau_DB.twb) with five linked worksheets built on a live Excel connection, confirmed via the rendered dashboard: KPI strip, Profit-vs-Sales scatter with trendline, Budgeted-vs-Actual Margin trend, Marketing Cost/Sales-per-Product combo chart, and a US state-level profit map.\n\nInsights Reading directly off the dashboard's own output:\nTotals: Total Sales 15,892; Total Revenue 8,298; Total Profit 799; Total COGS 7,594; Total Expenses 7,243; Average Marketing Cost 18. Profit is thin relative to revenue - roughly 10% of revenue converts to profit once COGS and expenses are accounted for.\nThe profit-vs-sales trendline is essentially flat to slightly negative across the plotted points - more sales in this dataset doesn't reliably mean more profit, which is exactly the kind of pattern a scatter-plus-trendline is meant to surface rather than hide inside a total.\nMarketing spend and sales don't move together consistently by product. Caffe Mocha gets the highest marketing spend and also does well; but Columbian shows the highest sales line value while sitting mid-pack on marketing spend, and Chamomile is low on both. There's no single story (\"more marketing = more sales\") that holds across every product.\nProfit by state is heavily concentrated. California leads clearly (31,785), with an Illinois-area state close behind (30,821); several other states sit far lower, in the roughly 8,000–17,000 range, and one state shows a notably low value (799) relative to the rest of the map.\n\nBusiness implication: The flat/negative profit-vs-sales trend is the headline finding here: it's a concrete illustration of why \"grow sales\" isn't automatically a profit strategy, and why a business would want product- and state-level views (not just one total) before deciding where to invest marketing dollars or expect returns.\n\nLimitations: Course dataset, not verified real sales data. Solo assignment work (brief requires individual student-ID submission, consistent with the Power BI and Analytica assignments). One visual - the Budgeted vs. Actual Margin trend by month, renders its month-axis labels but no visible line data in this screenshot; worth checking in Tableau directly before publishing, since I can't confirm from this image alone whether that's a filter state, a missing field, or just how it renders at this zoom level.",
