      "id": "315d8635-d500-4308-9447-4bf330d2c429",
      "title": "Financial Market Performance Dashboard (Power BI)",
      "type": "CASE STUDY",
      "summary": "I built a one-page Power BI dashboard that tracks profitability, liquidity, and valuation across eight companies at once - slicers, gauges, KPIs against goals, and trend charts all on a single screen.",
      "body": "Context: This was a Power BI assignment with a defined brief: given a financial-statements dataset, build a single-page dashboard to a specific visual spec — set slicers, gauges, KPI cards, and charts, each mapped to named fields — then submit the file under my own student ID.\n\nThe problem: Raw financial data across eight companies and roughly two decades (2005–2025 in the data) isn't usable at a glance. The brief was to turn that into something a decision-maker could scan in seconds: how profitable are these companies, are they hitting liquidity/cash-flow targets, and how does their market value compare over time.\n\nData / Input: An Excel dataset (\"Financial Statements\") with per-company, per-year records covering: EPS, Net Profit Margin, ROA, ROI, ROE, Gross Profit, Net Income, Free Cash Flow per Share (plus a goal), Current Ratio (plus a goal), Debt/Equity Ratio (plus a goal), the three cash flow components plus Net CF, Number of Employees, Shareholder Equity, Market Cap, and Revenue, across eight companies (AAPL, AIG, AMZN, BCS, GOOG, INTC, MCD, MSFT, among the entries shown). This is a course-provided dataset — some figures (e.g., employee counts in the millions) point to it being a synthetic/sample dataset rather than verified real-world company data.\n\nApproach: I mapped each required visual to the fields the brief specified: three slicers (Category, Company, Year — Year as a between-range slider), two gauges (EPS, Profit Margin — each showing average, min, max, and a target marker), five summary cards (ROA, ROE, ROI, Gross Profit, Net Income), three KPI visuals comparing each metric against a defined goal (Free Cash Flow per Share, Debt/Equity Ratio, Current Ratio), a multi-line cash flow chart by year, a donut chart of employee count by company, a horizontal bar chart of shareholder equity by company, and a combo chart overlaying market cap (stacked columns) and revenue (line) by year and company.\n\nAnalysis: Beyond just placing the visuals, this meant getting the right aggregation for each one — averages for ratio-type metrics (ROA, ROE, EPS, Profit Margin) versus sums/totals for scale metrics (Gross Profit, Net Income, Shareholder Equity, Market Cap) — and setting up the three KPI visuals to calculate and display percentage variance against a fixed goal rather than just showing the raw number.\n\nOutput A working, single-page Power BI report with 16 visual elements on one canvas, fully interactive - every card, chart, and gauge updates together when a slicer is changed.\n\nInsights Reading directly off the dashboard's own output:\nProfitability is healthy on average but not uniform. Average Profit Margin sits at 13.68% against a -44.70 to 36.69 range across companies/years, and average ROA is 12.43 - meaning some companies are running well below the group average.\nTwo of the three KPI targets are being missed by a wide margin. Average Free Cash Flow per Share (-0.76) is running about 478% below its goal of 0.20, and average Debt/Equity Ratio (-0.30) is roughly 115% off its goal of 2.00. Current Ratio is closer, at 1.13 against a goal of 1.50 (about 25% short).\nShareholder equity is concentrated at the top. Google and Microsoft lead the shareholder-equity ranking by a clear margin over the rest of the group, with the smallest company in the set barely registering on the same scale.\nMarket cap and revenue diverge after 2020. The market cap bars climb fairly steadily through the dataset, but the revenue line peaks around 2020–2021 and then drops sharply toward 2025, the two aren't moving together in the back half of the timeline.\n\nBusiness implication: This is the core value of a dashboard like this: it doesn't just report numbers, it flags where reality and target have drifted apart (the FCF and Debt/Equity KPIs) and where the underlying trend has changed direction (market cap vs. revenue post-2020) - the kind of thing a decision-maker would want surfaced automatically rather than found by digging through raw statements.\n\nLimitations: This is a course dataset, and some figures (employee counts in particular) suggest it's synthetic or scaled for the exercise rather than actual audited company data so the specific numbers shouldn't be read as real financial claims about these companies, only as a demonstration of the dashboard-building and metric-design process. Solo assignment work (the brief requires submission under an individual student ID).",
      "tools": "Power BI",
